// The plane a shadow receiver lies in, as a shadow map sees it.
//
// A receiver is compared, at each PCF tap, against its own plane's depth at
// that tap, so a flat surface needs no depth bias to keep from shadowing
// itself. That plane used to be read off the screen: dFdx and dFdy of the
// receiver's map position across a 2x2 quad of pixels. It was the pixel
// grid's answer, not the surface's - a quad that straddled two leaves, or a
// leaf's edge and the wall behind it, gave a slope at the clamp, and which
// pixels shared a quad changed with every turn of the camera and every
// flutter of a leaf. The shadow over foliage and along every contour was
// worked out afresh each frame, and it shimmered.
//
// Here the plane comes from the surface's own normal and the map's matrix
// alone, so it is the same whatever the camera does.
//
// test_shadow_plane.cpp compiles this file as C++ against glm, to run it:
// keep it to what both languages read the same way - no swizzles, no `out`
// parameters, no vector times a bare literal.

#ifndef SHADOW_PLANE_GLSLI
#define SHADOW_PLANE_GLSLI

// How far a receiver's map depth runs per unit of map uv, on the plane through
// it with normal `n` (world space, either side), in the orthographic map
// `space` (world to clip; uv = clip xy * 0.5 + 0.5, depth = clip z).
//
// An orthographic map is a rotation and a scale per axis, so the plane's
// normal in clip space is the world normal rotated and divided by each axis's
// scale: (M n) / s^2 componentwise, s the length of each row of M's 3x3.
// Along that plane depth changes by -(nx dx + ny dy) / nz, and a unit of uv is
// two of clip.
vec2 shadowPlaneSlope(mat4 space, vec3 n) {
    vec3 row0 = vec3(space[0][0], space[1][0], space[2][0]);
    vec3 row1 = vec3(space[0][1], space[1][1], space[2][1]);
    vec3 row2 = vec3(space[0][2], space[1][2], space[2][2]);
    // An orthographic map scales every axis by something, so no row is zero.
    vec3 m = mat3(space) * n;
    float nx = m.x / dot(row0, row0);
    float ny = m.y / dot(row1, row1);
    float nz = m.z / dot(row2, row2);
    // Edge-on to the light the plane runs away; past the clamp it is not lit
    // enough to matter, and an unbounded slope would push the taps off the map.
    float z = abs(nz) > 1e-12 ? nz : (nz >= 0.0 ? 1e-12 : -1e-12);
    float k = -2.0 / z;
    return clamp(vec2(nx * k, ny * k), vec2(-64.0), vec2(64.0));
}

#endif
